#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189207} :activity/id #uuid "d3179e6a-7442-53e2-9f07-f6ef555a8fc0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048165 :topic/id #uuid "84da0476-daa2-55f1-a6ab-9f6aa7e0bcf3" :topic/math-academy-id 3940} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189207 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189413 :tutorial/content "Given two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the variance of the sum or difference of these variables is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y],\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ We'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\nNote that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]= 0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y].\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "84bffdf5-551d-52b7-93f3-5c7ba599e972" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6496 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91e3c205-114c-5ae0-8abd-5bb7f424476e" :step/math-academy-id 39341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189208}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189208 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188357 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188065 :question/id #uuid "7ea98738-258e-5cc4-9e04-58b0b4638290" :question/math-academy-id "e-10435"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91379d24-6b6d-5642-8eb8-cbcc8feaf355" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050351}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527208}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum or Difference of Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "8ceb42c5-5f3d-52e4-b3c8-bda0bdf99ba6" :step/math-academy-id 39343 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189211}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189210 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188359 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188067 :question/id #uuid "0bc5206c-e880-5016-9653-49ac54af4d2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10443"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96172470-bd90-53eb-aea7-87d08f462ea2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527210}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "982300e3-8874-58e0-b502-0dd1759d2e87" :step/math-academy-id 39369 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189212}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189211 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189414 :tutorial/content "If $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab \\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0,$ and we recover the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] .\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a2319f43-f4e9-5092-93cf-5e5f4f23f4f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6502 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "8153054a-c500-5f13-a328-66eb26fc4fb8" :step/math-academy-id 39370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189210}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189212 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188360 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188068 :question/id #uuid "04bff442-bf0b-529b-a8d8-14538a1db48f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10444"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39efc83e-6658-5b7a-979b-dbc35af5dd98" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050327}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527224}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Combined Variance Given Some Moments"} :step/id #uuid "ba8e9c50-818a-52c4-8395-b662c6ac186d" :step/math-academy-id 39371 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189222}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189213 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188361 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188069 :question/id #uuid "d849af65-721a-5a47-b8b7-9446e77046f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-10447"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f512d235-f663-502f-bd8c-6b3b6748913b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048820}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527219}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum of Three Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "5fad4c43-2456-5538-bc40-e43bafabf67e" :step/math-academy-id 39377 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189223}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189222 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189420 :tutorial/content "Finally, we have the following generalization for the variance of a sum of $n$ random variables.\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var} \\left[ \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i X_i \\right] = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\textrm{Var}[X_i] + 2\\sum\\limits_{i < j} a_i a_j \\textrm{Cov}[X_i, X_j]\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how this works in practice." :tutorial/id #uuid "5169e0a6-5da8-51b4-8e84-5154fe8622f5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6535 :tutorial/title "Generalizing to Multiple Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "63b7b972-cc52-5658-b0d3-7a5571d28c29" :step/math-academy-id 39481 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189213}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189223 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189421 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X+ Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of variance and using the linearity of expectation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[((X+Y)-\\text{E}[X+Y])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[((X-\\text{E}[X])+(Y-\\text{E}[Y]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}+(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}+2(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDistributing the expected value, and using the fact that $\\textrm{E}[aX] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X]$ for constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}+(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}+2(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}]+\\text{E}[(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}]+2\\cdot \\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]+2\\text{Cov}[X,Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nThe proof for the difference between two random variables is similar." :tutorial/id #uuid "7523c9e9-51f7-52f5-b206-eec1940bf0f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6536 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Sum Formula"} :step/id #uuid "9262448d-d4d1-5e9c-a798-bcc9d934f487" :step/math-academy-id 39482}] :activity/title "Variance of Sums of Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188522}]}
